From: nullptr-t-oss <127822226+nullptr-t-oss@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 6 Oct 2026 02:40:00 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] certs: fix extract-cert failing to build with a stock OpenSSL

extract-cert is a host tool, built whenever CONFIG_MODULE_SIG_KEY is set
-- which gki_defconfig does (certs/signing_key.pem). It fails to compile:

  certs/extract-cert.c:152:7: error: use of undeclared identifier 'key_pass'
  certs/extract-cert.c:153:42: error: use of undeclared identifier 'key_pass'

This is an upstream regression, not something in this pipeline.

Upstream commit 64282a745897 ("extract-cert: Wrap key_pass with
'#ifdef USE_PKCS11_ENGINE'") wrapped the declaration at line 81 and the
assignment in main() with #ifdef USE_PKCS11_ENGINE, to silence a new
-Wunused-but-set-global warning under clang. Its rationale was that
"after commit 558bdc45dfb2, key_pass is only used with the OpenSSL
engine API, not the new provider API".

But the use was left in a different branch. In this tree the pkcs11:
branch is guarded only by #ifdef OPENSSL_IS_BORINGSSL:

  } else if (!strncmp(cert_src, "pkcs11:", 7)) {
  #ifdef OPENSSL_IS_BORINGSSL
          ERR(1, "BoringSSL does not support extracting from PKCS#11");
          exit(1);
  #else
          ...
          if (key_pass)
                  ERR(!ENGINE_ctrl_cmd_string(e, "PIN", key_pass, 0), ...);
  #endif

So for any host OpenSSL that is not BoringSSL, USE_PKCS11_ENGINE is not
defined (nothing in the kbuild system defines it -- grep over all
Makefiles finds no mention) while the branch that uses key_pass is
compiled, and the tool cannot be built at all. Before 64282a745897 the
declaration was unconditional and this compiled fine; that commit is
present in 6.6.142 and absent in 6.6.82, which is why the 6.6.82 tree
builds and this one does not.

This turns the guard into a three-way choice, which is what the upstream
commit clearly intended:

  #if defined(OPENSSL_IS_BORINGSSL)          unchanged behaviour
  #elif defined(USE_PKCS11_ENGINE)           unchanged behaviour
  #else                                      clear error, not a compile error

The new #else matters. Simply deleting the engine branch would let the
"pkcs11:" prefix match the else-if and then fall out of the if/else chain
with no output file written, which would leave signing_key.x509 empty
and only fail much later. Erroring out at extraction time names the
cause.

drain_openssl_errors() is guarded as well. It is called only from the
same engine branch (lines 145 and 149), so once that branch is compiled
out it becomes an unused static function. The current host build happens
to pass no -Wall/-Werror to hostprogs (KBUILD_HOSTCFLAGS is
$(KBUILD_USERHOSTCFLAGS) $(HOST_LFS_CFLAGS) $(HOSTCFLAGS) and HOSTCFLAGS
is unset), so this is only a warning today -- but it is the same latent
breakage, one compiler flag away from fatal, and leaving it would just
mean this patch needs redoing later.

Not affected by the new #else in practice: this kernel's signing key is
a plain file path, so the "pkcs11:" prefix is never matched.

Verified on this machine against OpenSSL 3.6.5, in all three
preprocessor configurations:

  stock OpenSSL, USE_PKCS11_ENGINE undefined   compiles clean with no
                                              flags, with -Wall, and with
                                              -Wall -Wextra -Werror
  USE_PKCS11_ENGINE defined                   compiles clean, no flags
                                              and with -Wall
  OPENSSL_IS_BORINGSSL defined                compiles clean

The "undeclared identifier" error reproduces on the unpatched file with
`gcc -o /dev/null certs/extract-cert.c -lcrypto` (exit 1, line 152).

Signed-off-by: nullptr-t-oss <127822226+nullptr-t-oss@users.noreply.github.com>
---
 certs/extract-cert.c | 23 ++++++++++++++++++++++++---
 1 file changed, 20 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

--- a/certs/extract-cert.c
+++ b/certs/extract-cert.c
@@ -56,7 +56,8 @@
 	}
 }

-#ifndef OPENSSL_IS_BORINGSSL
+#if !defined(OPENSSL_IS_BORINGSSL) && defined(USE_PKCS11_ENGINE)
+/* Only the ENGINE-based PKCS#11 path calls this. */
 static void drain_openssl_errors(void)
 {
 	const char *file;
@@ -128,10 +129,10 @@
 		fclose(f);
 		exit(0);
 	} else if (!strncmp(cert_src, "pkcs11:", 7)) {
-#ifdef OPENSSL_IS_BORINGSSL
+#if defined(OPENSSL_IS_BORINGSSL)
 		ERR(1, "BoringSSL does not support extracting from PKCS#11");
 		exit(1);
-#else
+#elif defined(USE_PKCS11_ENGINE)
 		ENGINE *e;
 		struct {
 			const char *cert_id;
@@ -154,6 +155,18 @@
 		ENGINE_ctrl_cmd(e, "LOAD_CERT_CTRL", 0, &parms, NULL, 1);
 		ERR(!parms.cert, "Get X.509 from PKCS#11");
 		write_cert(parms.cert);
+#else
+		/* The ENGINE-based PKCS#11 path above is the only user of
+		 * key_pass, and upstream commit 64282a745897 wrapped
+		 * key_pass's declaration in '#ifdef USE_PKCS11_ENGINE' while
+		 * leaving that branch guarded only by
+		 * '#ifdef OPENSSL_IS_BORINGSSL'. Nothing in the build system
+		 * defines USE_PKCS11_ENGINE, so with a stock OpenSSL host
+		 * build this file cannot be compiled at all. See
+		 * .github/actions/extract_cert_pkcs11_engine.patch.
+		 */
+		ERR(1, "PKCS#11 extraction requires USE_PKCS11_ENGINE");
+		exit(1);
 #endif
 	} else {
 		BIO *b;
